@ Index:   World time zones on a map (IANA tz database), with daylight saving time
@ Author:  Victor Valdes
@ Version: 0.1.0
@ Source:  https://github.com/valdesvj/zones47
@ Tested:  C47 simulator (python/c47sim.py): tests/test_zone47.py, every stop, its cities and the DST rules
@ Tested:  against Python's zoneinfo (the computer's tzdata) for many dates
@ Input:   the variable TZ, the offset of the calculator's clock from UT in hours (4 for UT+4, -5.5 for
@ Input:   UT-5:30); asked at the start when it is 0 or missing (R/S keeps it)
@ Output:  the map with the column of the stop highlighted; its offset, time and date; the clock (TZ) and the
@ Output:  difference; the city and its DST
@
@ ---- What it is ----
@ Zones 47 shows the world's time zones on a dot map of the world. Each standard offset of the official IANA time
@ zone database (from UTC-12 to UTC+14) is a stop; at each stop it shows the time and date there, the cities of every
@ country that uses it, and each city's daylight saving time, worked out for today's date (north and south). The
@ calculator's own zone is marked, so you can read the difference at a glance.
@
@ ---- How to use it ----
@ Load ZONE47 and press XEQ ZONE47. The first time it asks TZ, the offset of your calculator's clock from UT in hours
@ (4 for UT+4, -5.5 for UT-5:30, summer time included); R/S keeps the value shown. It is kept for the next runs.
@   4         the stop to the west (a smaller offset)        5 or 6   the stop to the east
@   8 2       the city before / after in the stop (each with its own daylight saving)
@   0         the end
@ The header shows the stop's offset, its time and date, your clock and the difference; under the map the city, its
@ country and its daylight saving now. The column of the stop is highlighted and a triangle marks your own zone. The
@ time updates every minute while it waits for a key.
@
@ ---- What it changes on your calculator ----
@ Your stack and LASTx are given back at the end. It uses the registers R20-R84 and clears them at the end; R00-R19
@ and R85-R99 are yours. It keeps one variable, TZ (your clock's offset; Almanac 47's MOON47 reads the same TZ).
@ GRMOD 0 and GRFNT 20 are set at the end.
@
@ ---- Programs and labels ----
@ One program, ZONE47, with one global label (ZONE47). The stops (LBL 01-nn, called with XEQ IND), the daylight
@ saving rules and every routine are local labels. Written with the STRUCT commands, ATEXT and GRFNT of the C47 / R47
@ firmware (00.109.05.00a0.ALPHA of 5 Oct 2026 or later). The cities and rules come from the computer's tzdata when
@ the program is built. A city can be added in the program (its stop's list of cities); take care with its daylight
@ saving rule and the counts, and keep a copy first.
@
@ ---- Technical notes (from the commented source) ----
@ ZONE47 v0.1.0 - Zones 47: the world time zones on the C47 / R47
@ A dot map of the world (3 degrees a dot, 81N to 54S) in 24 columns of 15 degrees, UTC-11 at the left
@ to UTC+-12 at the right (the last column: 172.5E to 172.5W). The official time zones: one stop per
@ standard offset of the IANA tz database (tzdata, zone1970.tab; tools/make_zones.py -> python/zones.py),
@ -12 (at sea) to +14, with a city and its daylight saving rule (DST: worked out for the date, added when on).
@ The column of the stop is highlighted (XOR); the header shows its offset, time and date, and the
@ calculator's clock (TZ) with the difference; under the map the city and its DST.
@ Keys: 4 the stop west (a smaller offset), 5 the stop east (6 too), around at the ends; 8 2 the city
@ before / after in the stop (every country of the offset, from zone.tab, each with its DST); 0 the end.
@ The time is updated every minute while the program waits for a key.
@ TZ: the global variable "TZ" = the offset of the calculator's clock from UT in hours (4 for UT+4, -5.5
@ for UT-5:30; the clock's offset as it is, summer time included), as MOON47 in Almanac 47: 0 STO+ "TZ"
@ makes it (0) when it is not there; TZ = 0 (new, or a clock on UT): asked with INPUT, R/S keeps it. The cursor
@ starts on the stop nearest to TZ, its column marked by a triangle.
@ Screen 400 x 240, row 0 = bottom (as in Almanac 47). ATEXT in GRFNT 21, the small text in GRFNT 10.
@ GRMOD and GRFNT take X (rejig writes "GRMOD r" as GRMOD and the number r: Almanac 47).
@ The user's stack and LASTx are kept (local registers R.00-R.08: STOS R.00, RCLS R.00); at the end the
@ work registers R20-R84 are cleared (R-CLR), the other registers are the user's.
@ Map: dot column j (0-119) at x 20 + 3j and 21 + 3j, dot row i (0 the top) at rows 194 - 3i, 193 - 3i.
@ Map column c (0-23: UTC-11 ... UTC+-12) at x 19 + 15c to 33 + 15c, rows 59-196.
@ The stops: LBL 01-nn (written by the build), each pushes the city (text), DST, the column, the offset.
@ Registers: R20-R22 the three bands of a map column (rows 61, 106, 151 up), R23 64 ones, R24 10 ones,
@ R25 the drawing column x, R26 the tick (3 rows), R27 a counter, R30 TZ, R31 the JD of UT now,
@ R28 the offset of the stop, R29 its column, R32 the stop (1-nn), R33 the key, R34 the text, R35 the JD
@ in the zone, R36 the minute shown, R37 the home stop, R38-R41 work, R42 the day and month names, R43 nn
@ (the number of stops), R44-R47 the date, R48 the city, R49 its DST (h), R50 its rule, R53-R58 and
@ R63-R71 the DST work, R59 the offset now, R60-R62 day month year.
@ Control flow in the C47 STRUCT commands (IF / ELSE / ENDIF, DO / WHILE / ENDDO: the firmware's app
@ note AN0007), indented two spaces per level; tools/build_zone47.py checks them and numbers them.
@

LBL "ZONE47"
@ the user's stack in the local registers: STOS R.00 (R.00-R.07, the 8 levels), then LASTx in R.08
LocR 9
STOS R.00
LASTx
STO R.08
XEQ 83                          ; -> :INIT:
@
@ ---- keys: 4 west, 5 or 6 east, 0 the end; other keys ignored ----
DO 01
@   wait for a key: PAUSE 50, KEY? 33 (true: no key yet), as Almanac 47; the clock read each time
  DO 02
    XEQ 70                      ; -> :TICK:
    PAUSE 50
    KEY? 33
  WHILE 02
  ENDDO 02
  RCL 33
  82
  X≠Y?
WHILE 01
  RCL 33
  62
  X=Y?
  XEQ 00                        ; -> :WEST:
@   5 (63) or 6 (64): |k - 63.5| = 0.5 for these two keys only
  RCL 33
  63.5
  -
  ABS
  1
  X>Y?
  XEQ 40                        ; -> :EAST:
@   8 (53) the city before, 2 (73) the city after, in the stop
  RCL 33
  53
  X=Y?
  XEQ 62                        ; -> :CUP:
  RCL 33
  73
  X=Y?
  XEQ 63                        ; -> :CDOWN:
ENDDO 01
@
@ ---- 0: the end: R20-R84 cleared, GRMOD 0, GRFNT 20, the screen; the user's stack and LASTx back ----
20.65
R-CLR
0
GRMOD
20
GRFNT
CLLCD
RCL R.08
STO L
RCLS R.00
RTN
@
@ ---- LBL :WEST: one stop west: the highlight off, the stop - 1 (0 -> nn), the new stop ----
LBL 00                          ; :WEST:
XEQ 68                          ; -> :BAND:
RCL 32
1
-
X=0?
RCL 43
STO 32
XEQ 39                          ; -> :MOVED:
RTN
@ LBL :MOVED: the new stop: its record, the highlight on, the header
LBL 39                          ; :MOVED:
XEQ 58                          ; -> :REC:
XEQ 68                          ; -> :BAND:
XEQ 72                          ; -> :HEAD:
RTN
@
@ ---- LBL :EAST: one stop east: the stop + 1 (nn + 1 -> 1) ----
LBL 40                          ; :EAST:
XEQ 68                          ; -> :BAND:
RCL 32
RCL 43
MOD
1
+
STO 32
XEQ 39                          ; -> :MOVED:
RTN
